{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-20519",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1\\,$ and $\\,x=1$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical lines $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-1,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be higher than the point at $x=-1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/12e6a8036759b8bbcc2f4fbe4ae7f4f7e18278de65fbdc0faa99c919f7234284.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and is increasing $↗$ on $(-1,1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-1,1)$ and is decreasing $↘$ on $(1,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and the right of $x=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/38f1c92810a3311ed939a54ac2856cb7ada111dbf24528785f80bd5f9ca49c84.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/7921a107b33a9b9bdc3df8dec418828f4cd5de2d89a9e7ab41845b0c64bbb653.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$",
